WEBVTT

00:00:00.124 --> 00:00:01.458
Food, or a hug?

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:04.728
In the 1950s, many experts said babies

00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:07.113
love their mothers because they feed them.

00:00:07.123 --> 00:00:09.913
Psychologist Harry Harlow
tested it with baby

00:00:09.923 --> 00:00:12.892
rhesus monkeys and two fake mothers.

00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:15.092
In one version, one was bare wire

00:00:15.102 --> 00:00:17.377
and held the only bottle of milk.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.666
The other was just soft cloth.

00:00:19.676 --> 00:00:21.552
The babies were taken from their mothers

00:00:21.562 --> 00:00:24.302
at birth, and Harlow's
experiments are still

00:00:24.312 --> 00:00:26.444
debated on ethical grounds.

00:00:26.454 --> 00:00:28.157
So where did the hungry babies spend

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:29.133
their days?

00:00:29.143 --> 00:00:30.915
Clinging to the soft one, most of

00:00:30.925 --> 00:00:31.887
the day.

00:00:31.925 --> 00:00:33.652
They visited the wire one only to

00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:34.637
drink.

00:00:34.675 --> 00:00:36.027
Harlow's answer:

00:00:36.037 --> 00:00:38.684
love runs on comfort, not food.

00:00:38.694 --> 00:00:41.667
It influenced thinking about
childcare and adoption,

00:00:41.677 --> 00:00:44.502
and later, how animal research is judged.

00:00:44.564 --> 00:00:45.202
So:

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:47.002
food, or a hug?
